WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.466
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.766 --> 00:00:08.851
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.151 --> 00:00:17.318
Unit twenty-five: Cities, Ruins and Time. In this unit, you will reflect on how cities remember and forget their past.

4
00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:19.945
urban archaeology.

5
00:00:20.645 --> 00:00:25.721
urban archaeology means the study of past human activity beneath modern cities.

6
00:00:26.021 --> 00:00:30.540
Urban archaeology revealed an ancient canal under the market.

7
00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:34.310
Your turn. Say: urban archaeology.

8
00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:38.592
urban archaeology.

9
00:00:39.583 --> 00:00:40.631
palimpsest.

10
00:00:41.331 --> 00:00:46.198
palimpsest means something with layers of history visible beneath the surface.

11
00:00:46.498 --> 00:00:50.982
Phnom Penh is a palimpsest of royal, colonial and modern eras.

12
00:00:51.682 --> 00:00:54.358
Your turn. Say: palimpsest.

13
00:00:57.058 --> 00:00:58.047
palimpsest.

14
00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:00.457
ruin aesthetics.

15
00:01:01.157 --> 00:01:04.864
ruin aesthetics means the appreciation of ruins as beautiful.

16
00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:08.498
Ruin aesthetics attracted Romantic painters to Angkor.

17
00:01:09.198 --> 00:01:12.501
Your turn. Say: ruin aesthetics.

18
00:01:15.201 --> 00:01:16.585
ruin aesthetics.

19
00:01:17.583 --> 00:01:18.910
heritage erasure.

20
00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:24.419
heritage erasure means the destruction or loss of historic places and memories.

21
00:01:24.719 --> 00:01:27.322
Rapid development risks heritage erasure.

22
00:01:28.022 --> 00:01:31.278
Your turn. Say: heritage erasure.

23
00:01:33.978 --> 00:01:35.316
heritage erasure.

24
00:01:36.317 --> 00:01:37.631
collective forgetting.

25
00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:42.908
collective forgetting means a society gradually losing memory of its past.

26
00:01:43.208 --> 00:01:46.729
Demolishing old buildings can encourage collective forgetting.

27
00:01:47.429 --> 00:01:51.021
Your turn. Say: collective forgetting.

28
00:01:53.721 --> 00:01:55.164
collective forgetting.

29
00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:57.883
architectural memory.

30
00:01:58.583 --> 00:02:02.776
architectural memory means the way buildings preserve traces of the past.

31
00:02:03.076 --> 00:02:06.550
Old shophouses carry the city's architectural memory.

32
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.831
Your turn. Say: architectural memory.

33
00:02:13.531 --> 00:02:15.125
architectural memory.

34
00:02:16.117 --> 00:02:17.721
adaptive heritage.

35
00:02:18.421 --> 00:02:23.939
adaptive heritage means historic places given new uses while keeping their character.

36
00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:28.433
A colonial villa reborn as a café is adaptive heritage.

37
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:32.783
Your turn. Say: adaptive heritage.

38
00:02:35.483 --> 00:02:36.856
adaptive heritage.

39
00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:38.979
urban layers.

40
00:02:39.679 --> 00:02:44.372
urban layers means different periods of history built on top of each other in a city.

41
00:02:44.672 --> 00:02:47.252
Every street reveals urban layers.

42
00:02:47.952 --> 00:02:50.953
Your turn. Say: urban layers.

43
00:02:53.653 --> 00:02:54.828
urban layers.

44
00:02:55.817 --> 00:02:58.095
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:58.395 --> 00:03:03.822
Rithy, Phnom Penh changes so fast. Does the city remember its past?

46
00:03:05.117 --> 00:03:08.869
Well, it's not exactly sentimental about old buildings.

47
00:03:10.183 --> 00:03:13.321
A rather gentle way of describing the demolitions!

48
00:03:14.617 --> 00:03:18.593
True. Developers have been, shall we say, enthusiastic.

49
00:03:19.883 --> 00:03:25.203
Yet the city is still a palimpsest: royal, colonial, modernist and modern.

50
00:03:26.517 --> 00:03:30.690
For now. Heritage erasure is happening one shophouse at a time.

51
00:03:31.983 --> 00:03:35.980
Some old villas have become cafés: adaptive heritage.

52
00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:42.719
Which is wonderful, if a little ironic: we preserve the past to sell expensive coffee.

53
00:03:44.017 --> 00:03:48.280
Grammar focus: Irony and understatement (extended).

54
00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:52.333
Irony and understatement allow polite criticism.

55
00:03:52.633 --> 00:04:00.510
The city is not exactly sentimental about old buildings. Developers have been, shall we say, enthusiastic.

56
00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:02.925
It's wonderful, if a little ironic.

57
00:04:03.225 --> 00:04:08.847
Understatement often signals criticism politely; listeners infer the stronger meaning.

58
00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:13.779
Now read and listen. The City as Palimpsest.

59
00:04:14.079 --> 00:04:18.784
Cities are rarely built on empty ground; they are layered over their own past.

60
00:04:20.083 --> 00:04:25.984
Scholars sometimes describe them as palimpsests, where earlier histories remain partly visible.

61
00:04:27.283 --> 00:04:33.195
Urban archaeology uncovers these layers, from ancient canals to forgotten foundations.

62
00:04:34.483 --> 00:04:40.906
Phnom Penh's urban layers include royal, colonial, modernist and contemporary architecture.

63
00:04:42.217 --> 00:04:46.503
Rapid development, however, has led to significant heritage erasure.

64
00:04:47.817 --> 00:04:53.439
When buildings disappear, collective forgetting may follow, as architectural memory is lost.

65
00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:59.315
Adaptive heritage offers a compromise, giving old structures new life.

66
00:05:00.617 --> 00:05:06.064
The challenge is to let a city grow without making it, so to speak, forget its own name.

67
00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:10.118
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:10.418 --> 00:05:14.125
Something with layers of history visible beneath the surface is a blank.

69
00:05:14.425 --> 00:05:20.145
A, palimpsest. B, ruin aesthetics. C, heritage erasure.

70
00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:26.997
The answer is A: palimpsest.

71
00:05:28.483 --> 00:05:31.481
'The film was not exactly short' means it was blank.

72
00:05:31.781 --> 00:05:35.145
A, short. B, long. C, good.

73
00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:42.136
The answer is B: long.

74
00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:47.530
'The meeting was, shall we say, lively' probably means it was blank.

75
00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.147
A, calm. B, heated. C, boring.

76
00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.440
The answer is B: heated.

77
00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:01.669
Historic places given new uses are blank.

78
00:06:01.969 --> 00:06:08.189
A, adaptive heritage. B, collective forgetting. C, urban archaeology.

79
00:06:12.183 --> 00:06:14.984
The answer is A: adaptive heritage.

80
00:06:16.483 --> 00:06:20.364
According to the text, what may follow when buildings disappear?

81
00:06:20.664 --> 00:06:26.221
A, collective forgetting. B, more tourism. C, urban archaeology.

82
00:06:30.217 --> 00:06:33.029
The answer is A: collective forgetting.

83
00:06:34.517 --> 00:06:40.965
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should old buildings be preserved in fast-growing cities?

84
00:06:46.265 --> 00:06:53.141
Part two: Use understatement or irony once, give two arguments and a compromise.

85
00:06:59.441 --> 00:07:11.802
Developers are not exactly famous for their love of history. Yet old buildings carry identity and attract visitors. A sensible compromise is adaptive heritage: keep the façade, change the use.

86
00:07:12.102 --> 00:07:14.148
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:20.925
Great work today! You learned eight new words and irony and understatement (extended).

88
00:07:21.225 --> 00:07:25.991
Today's value is love of country. Love and take care of Cambodia.

89
00:07:26.291 --> 00:07:29.942
See you in Unit twenty-six. Goodbye!

90
00:07:30.242 --> 00:07:32.662
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

